# Introduction
## **The history of printers**

Printers have been around for centuries; 
they first started off as simple mechanisms used to mass print text such as newspapers or books. 
They are an important part of our society’s development throughout history and with time they became more complex and the way they worked was constantly changing. 
Printers have come a very long way from the first prototypes to the modern age, with better capabilities, such as printing in colour and the ability to print images. 
Printers also became a lot more common throughout history, with only specific printing companies having access to them at first, and now many households have access to a printer, 
even those who do not own a printer have access to one in some way or another, whether its at a library or school, university, work etc. Printers have become as common as computers, 
and they often go hand in hand with users often printing files from their computers. For example, the standard equipment that you could find in a modern office will always be computers and 
printers and as of the modern age, the two types of printers available are desktop printers, usually connected to a PC and freestanding photocopier-printers with their respective popularity within 
households and business settings. Overall, printing technology has come a long way since the invention of the first printing presses, evolving from simple, manually operated machines into the advanced digital 
printers that we use today. Their development has influenced many aspects of everyday life, including education, communication, business, art, and publishing. In this case study, 
we will be taking a deep dive into the history and evolution of the printer, exploring its applications in modern times, as well as its societal and cultural impact. 
We will also discuss how advancements in printing have changed the way people communicate, learn, work, and express their creativity, 
highlighting the importance of printers in both historical and modern contexts.


